From 632dfe413afb0cec848858d6bccdd7e12de19532 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 04:29:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] swscale/ppc: add AltiVec 16-bit-source
 hScale16To15/hScale16To19

Add hScale16To15_altivec/hScale16To19_altivec (libswscale/swscale.c:69-126
are the C references) and register them in ff_sws_init_swscale_ppc()
mirroring swscale.c's own dispatch: "if (c->srcBpc != 8) c->hyScale =
c->hcScale = c->dstBpc > 14 ? hScale16To19_altivec : hScale16To15_altivec;"
as the else of the existing 8-bit assignment, unconditional on cpu
flags beyond the function's own AltiVec check already done above it.

The product is u16 (src) x s16 (filter); classic AltiVec has no
mixed-sign multiply-sum. Uses vmsumuhm (vec_msum on unsigned shorts,
filter reinterpreted as unsigned) which computes sum(src*f) + 65536 *
(sum of src at negative-filter lanes) mod 2^32, with a second
accumulator (masked 0/1 vec_msum) tracking that correction term,
subtracted at the end -- exact because the true signed total (which the
caller's filter-design contract guarantees fits in int32, same as the
scalar C's own "int val") survives the mod-2^32 round trip bit for bit.
Vectorizes filterSize % 8 == 0 (8-wide taps, ALIGNED filter-row loads --
rows are 2*filterSize*i bytes into an at-least-16-aligned base, so every
8-tap step of such a row stays aligned) and filterSize == 4 (PPC's other
producible size: the row's 4 taps are copied into a zero-padded 8-short
local buffer, sidestepping the row's real alignment entirely, at the
cost of an intentional harmless over-read of 4 source samples --
already an accepted pattern here, see hScale_real_altivec's own
filterSize == 4 case). Any other filterSize (never produced by this PPC
build; checkasm's synthetic filterSize=12 exercises it) falls back to a
scalar loop identical to the C, correctness-only. Four outputs are
processed per iteration, each with its own corrected accumulator, then
one mergeh/mergel transpose gathers all four totals into a single
vector -- avoiding the per-output store-then-scalar-read idiom the
existing 8-bit hScale_real_altivec uses (a known-marginal pattern on
7450 per its own comment). store16 (byte-precise store for any dst
alignment) is duplicated from yuv2yuv_altivec.c into this file, the two
being independent translation units.

Validated with tests/checkasm/sw_scale.c's extended check_hscale
(two commits back): seeds 1, 42, 777, 5, 99 all pass (existing 8-bit
ALTIVEC hscale rows, yuv2yuv1/X, yuv2nv12cX, and the new 16-bit rows);
--bench --duration=100 shows an _altivec counterpart for every one of
the 144 hscale_16_* names (filterSize 4/8/16/32/40 genuinely vectorized;
filterSize 12 registered but running the scalar-fallback path
internally, as intended). Full suite (`checkasm 7777`, no --test): 1268
tests, all passed, reconciling exactly against the stated baseline of
1079 plus the 189 new named checks added by the first commit (45
yuv2packed422_1/2/X + 144 hscale_16_*); stages 2 and 3 add AltiVec
counterparts for existing names, not new names, so they don't change
this count. Both touched files (this one; yuv2yuv_altivec.c and
swscale_altivec.c from the previous commit) were compiled standalone in
the plain and -D_ARCH_PWR4 shapes with -Wall -Wextra: no new warnings.

